[SIZE="5"][B]Inventory Companion User Manual[/B][/SIZE]

Inventory Companion is an Elder Scrolls Online addon for inventory transfers, item tracking, currency monitoring, and capacity alerts. This guide explains the current in-game controls and workflows.

[SIZE="4"][B]Open the addon and tracking editor[/B][/SIZE]

[LIST=1]
[*]In ESO, open [B]Settings > Add-Ons > Inventory Companion[/B] to change addon options.
[*]Open [B]Manage Tracking[/B] under [B]Panels[/B] in the addon settings, the tracker panel's right-click menu, its edit icon, or the key assigned to [B]Show/Hide Tracking Settings[/B] under [B]Settings > Controls > Keybindings > Inventory Companion[/B].
[*]Assign [B]Show/Hide Item Tracking Panel[/B] in the same keybindings section to toggle the tracker HUD. This changes the same saved visibility setting as [B]Show Tracked Items Panel[/B]; the panel can still appear temporarily while editing or interacting with items.
[*]Use [B]Manage Tracking[/B] and [B]Manage Groups[/B] to switch views. Show and Notify column headings identify the clickable tracking summaries. Right-click the tracker HUD for quick display options.
[/LIST]

[SIZE="4"][B]Show currency and inventory resources[/B][/SIZE]

[LIST=1]
[*]In the addon settings, open [B]Currency Summary Panel[/B].
[*]For a currency, use its [B]Character[/B] and [B]Bank[/B] controls to choose which balances appear. Account-wide currencies do not have those location controls.
[*]Use the row's show/hide button to include or exclude the resource from the panel. Hidden rows appear under [B]Not shown in panel[/B]. Use the up/down arrows to change the order of shown rows.
[*][B]Show Empty Currencies[/B] keeps currencies with a zero balance in the panel. Turn it off to show only currencies with a positive balance in at least one selected location.
[*][B]Grid Columns[/B] changes the number of columns immediately without changing resource order. The default is three; the maximum reflects resources available at logon/reload, regardless of filtering. Reset Panel restores three columns and the existing resource defaults without moving panels.
[*]Drag the currency panel to move it. Right-click it to toggle whether zero-balance currencies are shown.
[/LIST]

[URL=../Resources/currency-panel-settings.svg]Schematic of Currency Summary Panel settings, including location, Notify, visibility, and ordering controls[/URL]

The Currency Summary Panel and [B]Standalone Progress Bars[/B] are independent. The progress-bar settings let you show or hide a separate bar for backpack space, bank space, or a supported capped currency. Drag a bar to reposition it. Its advanced settings control warning and danger thresholds and the normal bar color; shared alert colors control warning and danger colors.

[B]Panel positions while using other screens[/B]
The tracker, currency panel and resource bars that normally show remain visible while using keyboard inventory, banks, the furniture vault, merchants, fences, IC editors and the main settings menu. Normal placeholder text also remains visible. Disabled panels stay hidden.

Panels already clear of the screen stay in place. Actual overlap with ZOS UI can trigger correction, but touching its edge does not. Displaced panels stack beside the active window, preferring its right edge, then its left. When finding new positions, IC leaves at least 5 pixels between its panels where space permits. In very tight spaces, panels may overlap with staggered corners so you can drag them apart. Other addons' windows are not considered.

[B]Snap panels into place:[/B] Drop a panel near another visible IC panel to align their edges or dock them with a 5-pixel gap. If you drop one panel over another, it can move farther than usual to reach the nearest clear side. Hold [B]Shift[/B] as you release to keep the exact drop position, including deliberate overlap. IC does not later move panels just to enforce spacing between them.

Drag a panel while one of these screens is open to save a position for that screen and character within the selected settings profile. Closing the screen restores its normal position. IC editor tabs share one layout; banks, house banks, guild banks, the furniture vault, inventory, merchants, fences and settings each have separate layouts. Withdrawal and deposit share their interaction's layout. User-chosen positions survive reload/logout. Changes to visible UI or panel sizes can temporarily move obstructed panels, including manually positioned ones, without overwriting saved positions or interrupting dragging. Unobstructed panels stay put; reopening a screen starts from saved positions again. Full-profile copying includes these layouts. Settings-only resets preserve them; panel-position resets also clear the affected panel's scene layouts.

[SIZE="4"][B]What the Notify controls do[/B][/SIZE]

There are two different kinds of Notify settings:

[LIST]
[*][B]Item or group Notify:[/B] In the tracked-items editor, click [B]Notify[/B] to open [B]Notify Chat[/B] and [B]Notify HUD[/B] checkboxes for backpack/craft-bag gains. Select either destination, both, or neither. The detail beneath Notify reads Chat, HUD, Both, or None; None uses the normal inactive coloring. An item can notify without being displayed, even when the tracker panel is hidden. Individually tracked items and matching groups combine their destination requests into one notification per gain. Use the [B]Notify[/B] filter to find entries with either destination enabled.
[*][B]Currency or capacity Notify:[/B] In [B]Currency Summary Panel[/B] settings, check [B]Notify[/B] on a currency or on [B]Backpack space[/B] / [B]Bank space[/B] to receive an alert when that resource changes. Currency notifications work even if the currency row is hidden. These settings are separate from item and group notifications.
[/LIST]

The item editor's [B]Show[/B], [B]Notify[/B], [B]Inactive[/B], and [B]Untracked[/B] filters control which entries are listed for editing; they do not themselves enable or disable alerts. Click the label or checkbox to toggle a filter. [B]Untracked[/B] adds currently carried or banked items to the editor so they can be added to your tracked list.

Currency and capacity alerts combine rapid changes into a single update and suppress startup/login changes. To disable one type of alert, clear Notify for its row.

[SIZE="4"][B]Track an individual item[/B][/SIZE]

[LIST=1]
[*]Open the tracked-items editor.
[*]To add an item currently in your inventory, right-click its item slot and choose [B]Add to Tracked Items[/B]. Alternatively, enable [B]Untracked[/B], open Show or Notify on an item row, and change a checkbox to add it to the current list. Opening a box alone does not track an item.
[*]Click [B]Show[/B] to choose [B]Show backpack[/B] and [B]Show bank[/B] independently. Backpack includes craft-bag holdings with ESO Plus. Neither hides the item without disabling its notifications.
[*]The detail beneath Show reads Backpack, Bank, Both, or None. On the tracker, a bag icon identifies carried stock and a bank icon identifies bank-only holdings. Both shows separate bank and backpack counts, bank first, without a slash or combined total. Changes apply immediately. Click away or the same control to close a box; changing filters, scrolling, or leaving the editor also closes it.
[*]Click the remove button on an item row to remove that item from the tracking list.
[/LIST]

[URL=../Resources/tracked-items-editor.svg]Schematic of the tracked-items editor, including list selection, filters, and item options[/URL]

On the tracker HUD, left-click a group header to expand or collapse it. Right-click the HUD to show/hide empty tracked items, switch between single-line and multiline names, or open the editor. The settings button beside the title's edit button opens [B]Settings > Addons > Inventory Companion[/B] directly. Both editors have an [B]Other Settings[/B] button beside their title that closes the editor and opens the same settings page. Under [B]Item Tracker Panel[/B] settings, [B]Tracker Title Size[/B] adjusts the regular-weight title and both buttons together (default 20, range 16-28), without changing item rows. The tracker visibility toggle hides the panel in inventory, bank, and editor windows as well as during normal gameplay.

[B]Automatic tracker layout:[/B] Under [B]Item Tracker Panel[/B] settings, [B]Tracker Group Threshold[/B] chooses when the flat item list becomes categories. [B]Maximum Visible Rows[/B] limits the item area (default 20, adjustable from 5 to 50); the title is separate. Wrapped names consume one row per displayed line. If the list is too large, the tracker first uses single-line names without changing your saved multiline preference. Wrapping returns when it fits again.

Left-clicking a category saves your preference for it to be open or closed. Manually closed categories stay closed across inventory changes, settings changes, and UI reloads until you open them again. Automatic height adjustments are temporary and do not change these saved preferences. On initial layout or relevant content/settings changes, the tracker restores categories you prefer open when they all fit. Opening a category that still exceeds the limit temporarily closes all other categories; otherwise they remain untouched. Changing a category's matching or display settings can restore it only if you prefer it open and it fits alongside the others. If an inventory burst causes overflow, only the one growing category that was already open is retained; if several open categories grow or there is no unique candidate, all categories temporarily collapse.

A category that is too large on its own, an oversized flat list, or too many category headers uses scrolling. Categories with more than 200 eligible items display an overflow message instead of their item rows; narrow the group's patterns to display its items. Summary counts still include all matching items. Use the mouse wheel or scrollbar; the title and its buttons remain fixed. The panel keeps the same width when the scrollbar appears: item names become narrower and counts shift left to make room. The panel also stays within the available screen height.

[SIZE="4"][B]Create and edit a custom group[/B][/SIZE]

[LIST=1]
[*]Open [B]Manage Tracking[/B] and select [B]Manage Groups[/B].
[*]Select [B]+ New Group[/B]. A new group is created and its name field opens; enter a name and press Enter or move focus away to save it.
[*]Select [B]+ Add Pattern[/B] to add a matching rule. Enter an item-name pattern, then move focus away from the field to save it. A green deposit (IN) icon before each row identifies an Include rule; a red withdraw (OUT) icon identifies an Exclude rule. Hover over the icon for an explanation. Use the small swap button after the pattern to change its kind; hover over the button to see what it will do. Changing a rule's kind moves it to the Include section or the Exclusions section below. A group includes an item when it matches at least one Include pattern and no Exclude patterns. For example, include [B]*treasure map*[/B] and add [B]*cyrodiil*[/B] as an Exclude to keep Cyrodiil treasure maps out of that group. Exclusions narrow only this group; the item may still match other groups or be tracked individually. Matching ignores letter case, and [B]*[/B] matches any part of an item name.
[*]Return to [B]Manage Tracking[/B]. Click the group's summary under [B]Show[/B] to choose [B]Display items together[/B], [B]Show backpack[/B], and [B]Show bank[/B]. Grouping adds ", grouped" to the detail while the group is shown. The summary under [B]Notify[/B] independently selects Chat and HUD gain alerts. Changing a group does not overwrite individually tracked item settings.
[*]In [B]Manage Groups[/B], use the group action buttons to hide/unhide or delete a custom group. Resetting a predefined group restores only its name and pattern list to the current addon defaults. All display settings, including hidden state, are retained. The [B]Miscellaneous[/B] group is a special catch-all and does not have editable patterns.
[/LIST]

Only your edits to predefined groups are saved. Unmodified names, patterns, and display settings can follow updated defaults in future addon versions. Restoring an edited value to its default removes that override.

[SIZE="4"][B]Use AutoCategory with tracked groups[/B][/SIZE]

AutoCategory integration is optional and is disabled by default. It requires a compatible AutoCategory addon; the Inventory Companion settings page reports whether the integration is available. Enablement is account-wide; group choices and ordering follow the selected settings profile. Without AutoCategory, its editor navigation button is hidden. When AutoCategory is loaded but integration is off, the button opens an information label and enable toggle.

IC and AutoCategory may independently use account-wide or character-specific settings. On login, reload or a profile change, IC projects its selected groups and saved order into AutoCategory's currently selected profile. Stale IC-generated categories are replaced automatically; native categories and inactive profiles are preserved. First enabling character settings copies the account profile once. Later switches resume the retained character profile without merging account edits.

On first enablement, choose whether IC groups start [B]Near the top[/B] (the default) or [B]At the bottom[/B] of your existing categories. Groups with any Show or Notify option enabled are included automatically during setup. You can change their order and participation later. In Main Settings, expand [B]AutoCategory[/B] under [B]Integrations[/B] for its information, account-wide [B]Enable[/B] toggle and compact [B]Settings[/B] button. The same button opens recovery when the integration needs attention; there is no separate More information button.

AutoCategory inclusion is managed only in [B]Manage AutoCategory[/B], independently of Show, Notify and tracker visibility. [B]Manage Tracking[/B] has no AutoCategory column.

Open [B]Manage AutoCategory[/B] to choose Backpack, Bank, Guild Bank, House Bank, Crafting Station, Craft Bag, or Furniture Vault. [B]Edit Display Order[/B] controls where assignments appear; [B]Edit Priority Order[/B] controls which matching assignment wins first. Moving an entry changes only the selected list and context. Native AutoCategory assignments are included as rows. Pick another context under [B]Copy from[/B] and press [B]Copy[/B] to copy both orders for shared entries. Later changes do not stay linked. There is no cross-mode copy or divergence warning.

Moving an assignment does not rename its AutoCategory category. AutoCategory's native hidden state and other assignment settings are preserved. An AutoCategory error displays a warning and stops synchronization, while other Inventory Companion features remain available. Correct the reported problem, then use [B]Retry synchronization[/B] in the recovery panel. Retry and confirmed assignment repair invalidate cached item classifications so categories are checked again; hidden inventories update when next shown. Existing integration choices and saved category order are retained.

In the AutoCategory panel, click [B]Hide[/B] or [B]Show[/B] to change a row's inclusion in both order lists for the selected context only. For example, hide Rare Mats in Bank while keeping it shown in Craft Bag. IC groups and native categories share this behavior without deleting source groups, assignments or their options. Hidden rows remain below the excluded divider, with no movement controls. Showing a row inserts below the occupant of its remembered display position (or appends); its restored display number also supplies the matching-priority insertion reference.

[B]Other[/B] catches items after all included categories but before every excluded IC group or native category. Its special row is movable in Display only, including with multi-selection; it has no Hide/Show button and cannot be deleted or edited as a group. Moving it never changes matching precedence. Its position is saved independently in each context and it is absent from Priority. Cross-context copying includes its Display placement. Other does not replace a native AutoCategory category named Other. New IC groups start included in all seven areas, immediately above Other's current Display position and at the bottom of included Priority.

Click an included row to start a new selection containing just that row; hold Shift and click to add or remove rows from the current selection. Selected rows have a blue background. Clicking the only selected row keeps it selected. These selections are separate from inclusion labels. Excluded rows cannot be selected for movement.

Every orderable row has [B]^[/B] and [B]v[/B] buttons. On a selected row, these move the whole selection up or down one step. On an unselected row, they select and move just that row. A contiguous selected block moves past one neighbouring unselected row; separated selected runs each move one step without being gathered together. A run already at the boundary stays put. Relative order within the selected and unselected rows is preserved.

The bottom toolbar provides [B]Select block[/B], [B]Clear[/B], then [B]Up 1[/B], [B]Up 5[/B], [B]Top[/B], a gap, [B]Down 1[/B], [B]Down 5[/B], and [B]Bottom[/B]. Top and bottom gather the selection into one block. Five-step moves behave like pressing the one-step button five times, but queue only one final order change. Selection stays highlighted after movement, and the leading edge in the movement direction is brought into view.

[B]Select block[/B] adds all touching rows of the same type around every selected row, stopping at AC/IC boundaries. It can expand multiple separated same-type runs without selecting intervening rows of the other type. It is unavailable for mixed AC/IC selections. [B]Clear[/B] clears only the selection. Changing the context or Display/Priority mode clears selection so other-list selections cannot move unexpectedly. Selection also clears when changing the active account/character list.

Order changes apply to AutoCategory immediately.

AutoCategory failures are recorded automatically when diagnostic logging is enabled. When recovery is needed, [B]Log error[/B] records the failure, traceback and available diagnostic context even when background logging is off. It does not retry synchronization or change your settings. Feedback beside the recovery controls and in chat confirms recording or reports an unavailable diagnostic log. Run [B]/reloadui[/B] or log out to write the recorded error to the installation's SavedVariables file.

[B]Undo changes[/B] appears on Items, Groups and AutoCategory only while changes differ from that panel's opening state. It restores that visit's baseline; manually reversing edits also hides the button. Closing the panel, changing panels or opening Main Settings discards its in-memory baseline. Reopening starts a new visit. AutoCategory undo restores only the current context and order mode, plus the context's inclusion choices shared by both modes; it does not rewind the opposite order or other contexts. If a required native category has disappeared, undo reports the problem without a partial restore.

The recovery panel retains attempted recovery and a last-resort [B]Reset integration[/B]. Reset warns that the previous arrangement and native exclusions will be replaced, then asks for the starting arrangement. Cancelling either dialog preserves the current configuration. Confirming rebuilds the arrangement and clears native exclusions and remembered positions, while preserving groups, items, per-area IC inclusion choices and inactive profiles.

Disabling integration removes its generated categories, including its internal Other, but preserves your IC choices for re-enabling. IC exclusions will stop applying. AutoCategory keeps the last matching order set by IC; previously excluded categories may match later than others. IC preserves unique AC-side options on its generated assignments so returning groups can reuse them.

[B]Saved settings and collision repair:[/B] Exact display-name collisions between a custom and built-in group are repaired in the existing SavedVariables schema, preserving effective patterns, display settings and AutoCategory placement. The profile version is unchanged. A built-in group name that collides with any virtual group stops startup before saved settings are changed. Back up your saved variables before upgrading. DEV and release share these settings.

If saved settings require a newer version than the installed addon supports, the addon stops initialization with an error before loading or changing those settings. Install a compatible newer addon or restore a compatible backup.

[URL=../Resources/custom-groups-editor.svg]Schematic of the custom-groups editor, showing the group list and pattern rules[/URL]

[SIZE="4"][B]FCO ItemSaver test groups and diagnostics[/B][/SIZE]

FCO ItemSaver integration is off by default. In [B]Settings > Addons > Inventory Companion > FCO ItemSaver (planned)[/B], enable it only when FCO ItemSaver is loaded and ready. Enabling does not create groups. Suspend preserves the configuration while making its groups inactive; confirmed Turn Off removes all FCO-dependent groups and their owned references from all profiles and backups, without changing FCO ItemSaver's own marks or settings.

[B]Add test FCO groups[/B] and [B]Add overlapping FCO test groups[/B] are under [B]Debugging[/B], affect only the currently selected item profile and require confirmation. Both require integration to be enabled and FCO ItemSaver ready. The first creates Deconstruct, Sell to vendor and Sell at guild store as normal custom groups. The second adds groups for available enabled markers and mixed, multiple and negative marker conditions; unavailable markers are reported. Groups are shown in the tracker for the backpack only, grouped, with notifications off, and placed at the top of AutoCategory Priority and Display without enabling AutoCategory globally. Repeating either action preserves existing edits. Inactive groups are hidden in Manage Groups and Manage Tracking but remain in their saved positions in Manage AutoCategory, with their inactivity reason.

[B]Enable Diagnostic Logging[/B] controls background events and performance samples in SavedVariables. It is enabled by default in DEV and disabled by default in release builds. Explicit [B]/ictest[/B] runs and Debug Key reports save even when background logging is off, retaining the latest ten complete reports with timestamps and sequence numbers in a separate test-report section. Long report lines are stored in shorter consecutive chunks without omitting text. The [B]copy[/B] option remains accepted, but reports no longer open deliberately raised error messages. Run [B]/reloadui[/B] or log out before reading the reports from the installation's SavedVariables file. [B]Clear Diagnostic Log[/B] removes background events, samples and test reports after confirmation. Diagnostics and scanning do not alter inventory or unrelated saved settings.

The Debug Key does not run the long tracker benchmark. To run it explicitly, use [B]/ictrackperf[/B], or [B]/ictest trackerperf[/B] to include it in a saved test report. The benchmark performs 3,000 scans and may significantly reduce frame rate until it finishes.

[SIZE="4"][B]Use account-wide or character-specific settings[/B][/SIZE]

In Main Settings, [B]Use Character Settings[/B] switches the full selected profile, including tracked items, group rules, tracker layout, currency and progress-bar preferences and editor filters. First use copies the account profile; subsequent switches resume the retained character profile. The copy explanation sits above [B]Copy From[/B]. Under [B]Panels[/B], the three management buttons share one row. Integration options are grouped into expanders, and [B]Debugging[/B] is the final expander at the bottom.

To replace the active profile:

[LIST=1]
[*]Choose a source in [B]Copy From[/B].
[*]Select [B]Copy Full Profile[/B] and confirm the replacement.
[/LIST]

Copying replaces the full destination profile, never merges, and removes destination-only profile settings. Integration enablement, transfer delay, auto-merge chest stacks, Debug Mode and diagnostic timing controls remain account-wide and are not copied. Identity, schema, caches and recovery data are preserved. Planned TTC/FCO controls are disabled and off; they do not enable integrations or store settings.

[SIZE="4"][B]Deposit and withdraw crafted items[/B][/SIZE]

Assign keys for [B]Deposit Crafted Items[/B] and [B]Withdraw Crafted Items[/B] under [B]Settings > Controls > Keybindings > Inventory Companion[/B]. Open a bank or storage chest, then use the relevant key. The addon queues supported items crafted by the current character and reports transfer progress. It will not transfer items outside its built-in crafted-item list.

[B]Auto-Merge Chest Stacks[/B] in the addon settings controls whether matching backpack stacks are automatically moved into house storage chest stacks when the chest opens. This setting does not enable merging for the regular bank.

[SIZE="4"][B]Move part of a stack[/B][/SIZE]

With a bank, subscriber bank, or house chest open, right-click a stack with more than one item. Choose [B]Deposit 1[/B] / [B]Withdraw 1[/B] or the corresponding option for all but one item. These actions move only that stack quantity and are separate from the bulk crafted-item keybindings.

Guild banks also offer partial deposit and withdrawal options when your permissions allow them. Partial deposits require guild-bank deposit access, one free backpack slot and one free guild-bank slot. Partial withdrawals temporarily withdraw the whole stack and return the remainder; they require both deposit and withdrawal access, at least two free backpack slots and a free guild-bank slot. The account-wide [B]Enable Partial Guild Bank Withdrawals[/B] setting controls withdrawals and is on by default.

[SIZE="4"][B]Adjust tracker and transfer settings[/B][/SIZE]

[LIST]
[*]In [B]Item Tracker Panel[/B] settings, [B]Show Tracked Items Panel[/B] is the master visibility switch. [B]Tracker Group Threshold[/B] determines when group headers are used; below the threshold, visible entries are shown as a flat alphabetized list. [B]Multiline Item Names[/B] and [B]Show Empty Tracked Items[/B] adjust the display.
[*][B]Item Transfer Delay (ms)[/B] controls spacing between queued bank transfers. Lower values may make transfers faster but can risk an ESO disconnect; keep the value conservative.
[*]In [B]Standalone Progress Bars[/B], each resource can be shown independently and has advanced threshold/color settings.
[/LIST]
